.TH ENV 3
.SH NAME
env \- environment variables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bind #e /env
.nf

.BI /env/ var1
.BI /env/ var2
\&...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I env
device serves a one-level directory of files with arbitrary names and
contents.
The file name is the name of an environment variable (see
.IR rc (1)),
and its content is the variable's current value.
.PP
Every process has its own directory, called its
.IR "environment group" .
A
.I fork
(see
.IR fork (2))
gives a new process the same group as its parent, so both see the same
files and a change made in one is visible in the other.
.B RFENVG
splits the group: from then on each side keeps its own copy, and a change
in one is invisible to the other.
.B RFCENVG
splits and starts the copy empty.
.I Exec
keeps whichever group a process already has, because the environment
belongs to the process rather than to the program running in it.
The last process holding a group frees its variables when it ends.
.PP
A value is up to 4096 bytes.
Creating a name makes it with an empty value.
A write at an offset overwrites and, past the current length, extends the
value.
An open with
.B OTRUNC
(see
.IR open (2))
empties it first, and a
.I wstat
of the length truncates or zero-fills it.
That is what lets
.B "echo x >/env/y"
replace a value and
.B >>
append to it, without the device knowing which a shell meant.
Removing a name unsets the variable.
.PP
A read or a write against a name that has been removed, or a fid opened on
one and then recreated under the same name, refers to nothing: an id is
never reused within a group, so a stale fid names no variable rather than
whatever came to occupy the name next.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/env
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (3),
.IR env (1),
.IR rc (1),
.IR fork (2),
.IR open (2),
.IR stat (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Creating a name that already exists fails with
.BR EEXIST .
A name longer than 64 bytes, or one containing
.B /
or a control character, fails with
.BR EINVAL .
A write that would put a value past 4096 bytes fails with
.BR ENOSPC .
